hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well in the middle of  meditation 6 Rene Descartes engages in a  little bit of discussion on the human  faculties and he's not attempting to  provide a comprehensive survey but he is  setting out some important  characteristics of the faculties of the  human mind and now at this point most  importantly not just the human mind but  also the human body so the human being  as a totality composed of two different  substances United in some as yet to be  explained way namely a thinking  substance that of the human mind and a  extended or a corporeal substance that  of the human body and there's the a  juncture between them and you can say  that certain of the faculties you might  say straddle the the two of these they  don't do so in exactly the same way so  that they're present in the mind in the  same way that they're present in the  body that would be impossible given the  nature of the mind and the nature of the  body one of which is divisible the other  which is not divisible one of which is  extended the other which is not extended  not material does not exist in space and  time but there are some  the faculties that you could say not  only make reference to something outside  of the human mind but actually involve  the human body and even more so involve  the human body in a world of other  bodies a world of extended substances  which act upon each other so he says and  this is uh about halfway through the  sixth meditation I find in myself  particular very particular faculties of  thinking which are distinct from me that  is the faculties of imagining and of  sensation  imagine a a sont here in French right  without which I would be able to  conceive myself clearly and distinctly  as a whole to taunt the air but not  those without me that is without a  intelligent substance to which they are  attached and he goes on he says the  notion that we have of these faculties  or their their formal concept in that  they they include some sort of  intellection from which i conceive that  they are distinct from me just like  figures and movements and other modes or  accidents of bodies are from the bodies  themselves which maintained or underlie  them su Tian right and so as opposed to  say the faculties that are more purely  of the human mind like intellection or  conception the work of the understanding  the untundi MA in French or that a  volition which by this point in the  meditations we've seen encompasses an  awful lot not just selection but also  affirming denying you know engaging in  judgments pursuing and avoiding engaging  in practical decision-making in action  which also includes judgments and then  we might also include the Faculty of  memory although we have to be a little  be careful with that right because their  Faculty of memory includes not only  purely intellectual things like  remembering the coach ito or remembering  the arguments that that decart is  provided in this but it also includes a  lot of things coming from sensation and  so memory is a little bit of a hybrid  compared to volition and intellection  but even those you could say  intellection is oftentimes going to be  of things that we are imagining or  sensing we're grasping the true and  immutable natures of them as we saw in  meditation five volition is another  thing which extends outward into the  world of our body and other bodies so we  could say that there's something as well  of that but we can conceive of this  stuff over here without bringing in the  body as decart did in the earlier  meditations when he stripped away the  body when he stripped away the entire  universe by means of the hyperbolic  doubt not so with imagination and  sensation and so he goes on and he says  here we go we also have other we also  have other faculties he says like those  of changing place or of putting  ourselves into multiple positions or  postures right and other similar things  which cannot be conceived he says any  more than the preceding ones and the  preceding ones are imagination  and sensation without some substance to  which they would be attached and  therefore by consequence cannot exist  without them then he goes on he says but  it's very evident that these faculties  if it's true that they exist must be  attached to some corporeal or extended  substance and not to a intelligent  substance why because in their clear and  distinct concept there is some sort of  tension which is found included in that  but not at all that of intelligence so  you know all these things that have to  do with what we do with our body you  know changing place moving from one spot  to another locomotion as we say we might  also think about the things within our  body like the Faculty of digestion right  or you know things like that all of  those really pertain just to the body  now you might say volition could play a  role in that right we move from one  place to the other because we decide to  do that right inside my intelligence or  understanding and my intellect there's  some sort of process that's going on  that says hey move that body of yours  from one place to another but the actual  Faculty of locomotion is just there